{#
_grid.html.twig — the calendar itself, and the one place that picks a view's
layout.

Rendered by the docked pane and by the full page, and it renders **the same
thing for both**. There is one calendar in this application; the pane is a
narrower window onto it, not a lesser version of it.

It was not always. Day and week used to branch on `isPane`: the page got the
time-grid and the pane got a column list, on the reasoning that seven columns of
positioned blocks behind a time gutter need roughly a screen's width and the
pane had ~380px of a row it shared with the mail. That reasoning was right about
380px and wrong about the pane, for two reasons that only became true later. The
pane is resizable to CALENDAR_PANE_MAX_WIDTH and can now be dragged past it into
a full-width calendar (see ui--split), so its width is the user's choice rather
than a constant to design around. And a pane that quietly draws a *different*
calendar is a worse answer to a narrow column than a grid that is tight in one:
the timeline is the thing the view is for, and somebody who widened the pane
specifically to see it got the list anyway.

What is left of the old argument is honest and stated in _time_grid_shell, which
draws the grid for this calendar and for the public shared one alike: a
seven-day grid below about 640px is tight. The toolbar's Day view is one tap
away and is a single full-width column, which is the answer for a narrow pane
and for a phone alike.

CalendarView::isTimeGrid() is now the whole of the decision: which views have a
time axis worth drawing at all.

The scroll container differs between the two branches on purpose. Month and
agenda scroll as one block, so the wrapper owns the overflow. The time-grid
keeps its day headers and its all-day band pinned while only the hours scroll,
so it owns its own scrolling and this wrapper must not add a second scrollbar
around it.

`isPane` survives as context rather than as a branch here: _view_month reads it
to keep its "N more" link inside the pane's frame.

── The calendar's entrance, and why it is here and nowhere below ─────────────

`data-enter="fade"` on the wrapper, so that changing the date or the view
crossfades the grid instead of cutting to it. Paging a week and switching
day/week/month are the two things people do repeatedly in here, and a hard cut
between two grids that look almost alike is the case animation is actually for:
the eye cannot tell "this redrew" from "this never changed" without it.

**On the WRAPPER, and never on what is inside it.** One element fading is one
gesture at one duration however many events are on screen — the alternative,
marking every chip or every month cell, is forty-two animations on a month and
a list that assembles itself while somebody is already reaching for the tenth
switch of the afternoon. It also keeps every entrance off the time-grid's
positioned blocks, which is not a stylistic preference: calendar--time-grid
writes `style.transform` and `style.height` on a block during a drag and reads
its geometry back with getBoundingClientRect(), and a CSS animation on that
element wins over the inline transform in the cascade — the block would lag the
pointer and the controller would measure a half-finished frame. Opacity on an
ancestor changes no box and no transform, so the drag cannot see this at all.

**fade, not rise.** A grid that slid into place would move every hour line and
every block a few pixels on every navigation, over a view whose whole job is
that a thing is drawn exactly where it is. Nothing here should appear to be at
07:14 for a sixth of a second.

Deliberately NOT in the three shells. _month_shell, _agenda_shell and
_time_grid_shell are rendered by the public shared-calendar page too, and that
page's layout writes no `data-motion` — it would take the Full-tier fallbacks
whatever the reader asked for. Motion opts in from the caller that has a tier.
#}

{% set days = range.days %}

{% if view.isTimeGrid %}
    <div class="flex-1 min-h-0 flex flex-col" data-controller="calendar--grid" data-enter="fade">
        {% include 'calendar/_view_timegrid.html.twig' %}
    </div>
{% else %}
    <div class="flex-1 min-h-0 overflow-y-auto" data-controller="calendar--grid" data-enter="fade">
        {% if view.value == 'month' %}
            {% include 'calendar/_view_month.html.twig' %}
        {% else %}
            {% include 'calendar/_view_agenda.html.twig' %}
        {% endif %}
    </div>
{% endif %}
